\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In a Lindahl mechanism, we apply different prices of the public good to
 different consumers.
 The idea of the Lindahl solution is that the consumption level of a public
 good is the same to all consumers, but the price of the public good is
 personalized to achieve the Lindahl-Samuelson condition.
 For simplicity, we normalize the price of the private good to 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agent 1 wishes to maximize his utility subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{1}(x_{1},y)=x_{1}y\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{1}+q_{1}y=20
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

After substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{20-x_{1}}{q_{1}}$
\end_inset

 into the utility function, we take the derivative of the function with
 respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{1}$
\end_inset

 to derive the first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\frac{20}{q_{1}}-2\frac{x_{1}}{q_{1}} & = & 0\\
x_{1} & = & 10
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Agent 2 wishes to maximize his utility subject to his budget constraint:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{2}(x_{2},y_{2})=x_{2}y^{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & x_{2}+q_{2}y=20
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

After substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{20-x_{2}}{q_{2}}$
\end_inset

 into the utility function, we take the derivative of the function with
 respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{2}$
\end_inset

 to derive the first-order condition
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\left(\frac{20-x_{2}}{q_{2}}\right)^{2}-\frac{2}{q_{2}}x_{2}\left(\frac{20-x_{2}}{q_{2}}\right) & = & 0\\
\frac{2}{q_{2}}x_{2} & = & \frac{20-x_{2}}{q_{2}}\\
x_{2} & = & \frac{20}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, the amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 produced is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
y_{1}=\frac{1}{2}v_{1}=\frac{1}{2}(20-10)=5\\
y_{2}=\frac{1}{2}v_{2}=\frac{1}{2}(20-\nicefrac{20}{3})=\frac{20}{3}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part d.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This production and allocation satisfies the conditions for Pareto effiiciency
 since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
2x_{1}+3x_{2}=2(10)+3(\nicefrac{20}{3})=40
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider an economy with two goods, a private (rivalrous) good 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, say leisure, and a public (non-rivalrous) good 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, say radio broadcase music.
 Both goods are measured in hours per day.
 There are two consumers, 1 and 2, and one firm.
 The firm produces 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

, using labor 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 as input.
 (Labor is leisure given by a consumer to the firm; hence, if consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 supplies 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 units of labor to the firm, then the amount of leisure left to 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=w_{i}-v_{i}$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's initial endowment of leisure.) Let the produciton function of the firm
 be linear (constant returns to scale), with 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 units of 
\begin_inset Formula $v$
\end_inset

 needed to produce one unit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 at any scale of output 
\begin_inset Formula $(k>0)$
\end_inset

.
 There is no free disposal.
 The inital endowments 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}$
\end_inset

 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 are positive, but the inital endowment of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Assume that each consumption set consists of all points 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq0$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
 Furhtermore, for any 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq0$
\end_inset

, the utility function of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is givne by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=x_{i}+\phi_{i}(y)
\]

\end_inset

where the valuation function 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}(y)$
\end_inset

 is twice differetiable, 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}^{\prime}(y)>0$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi^{\prime\prime}(y)<0$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $y\geq0$
\end_inset

, for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

.
