\begin_layout Standard
The conditions for Pareto efficiency in the presence of public goods are
 given by the feasibility constraints and the Lindahl-Samuelson condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}} & = & \frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}\\
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+v & \leq & \sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\
y & = & f(v)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y_{1}+y_{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v=v_{1}+v_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 So, the conditions for Pareto efficiency in this economy are as follows
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\phi_{1}^{\prime}(y)+\phi_{2}^{\prime}(y)=k\\
x_{1}+v_{1}=w_{1}\\
x_{2}+v_{2}=w_{2}\\
ky=v_{1}+v_{2}=(w_{1}-x_{1})+(w_{2}-x_{2})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

In this economy, note that the Pareto optimality condition depends only
 on the aggregate amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 produced.
 It does not matter who contributes, so long as enough is contributed.
 So, in the case of quasi-linear utility functions, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is uniquely determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, let us find the Nash Equilibrium.
 In a Nash Equilibrium, each consumer takes the other agent's strategy as
 given, and maximizes his payoffs.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{x_{i}} & u_{i}=x_{i}+\phi_{i}(y)\\
\mbox{s.t.} & y=\frac{1}{k}(v_{i}+v_{-i})\\
 & x_{i}+v_{i}\leq w_{i}\\
 & v_{i}\geq0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Setting up the Lagrangian we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=x_{i}+\phi_{i}\left(\frac{1}{k}[v_{i}+v_{-i}]\right)+\lambda(w_{i}-x_{i}-v_{i})+\mu v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Taking partial derivatives we obtain the following first-order conditions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda & = & 1\\
\frac{1}{k}\phi_{i}^{\prime}(y)+\mu & = & \lambda\\
\lambda(w_{i}-x_{i}-v_{i}) & = & 0\\
\mu v_{i} & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}>0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu=0$
\end_inset

, and this implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}^{\prime}(y)=k$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}=0$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu\geq0$
\end_inset

, and this implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $\phi_{i}^{\prime}(y)\leq k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

