\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
(Lindahl Equilibrium) 
\series default
An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})\in\mathbb{R}^{3}$
\end_inset

 is a Lindahl equilibrium if it is feasible and there exists a price vector
 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and personalized price vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

, one for each individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,2$
\end_inset

, such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(i) 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}x_{i}^{*}+q_{i}^{*}y^{*}\leq p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(ii) 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)\succ_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 implies 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}x_{i}+q_{i}^{*}y>p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset

;
\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
(iii) 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}y^{*}-p^{*}v^{*}=0$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{*}=\sum_{i=1}^{2}w_{i}-\sum_{i=1}^{2}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{2}q_{i}^{*}=q^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*
We call 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*},p^{*},q_{1}^{*},q_{2}^{*})$
\end_inset

 a Lindahl Equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, we prove that every Lindahl-Equilibrium is Pareto optimal under local
 non-satiation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Suppose not.
 Then, 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset

there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{x}_{1},\hat{x}_{2},\hat{y})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i=1,2:(\hat{x}_{i},\hat{y})\succsim_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k\in\{1,2\}:(\hat{x}_{k},\hat{y})\succsim_{i}(x_{k}^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

.
 By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\hat{x}_{i}+q_{i}^{*}\hat{y}\geq p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 From the definition of a Lindahl-Equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{x}_{k},\hat{y})\succ_{k}(x_{k}^{*},y^{*})\implies p^{*}\hat{x}_{k}+q_{k}^{*}\hat{y}>p^{*}w_{k}$
\end_inset

.
 So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i=1}^{2}p^{*}\hat{x}_{i}+\sum_{i=1}^{2}q_{i}^{*}\hat{y} & > & \sum_{i=1}^{2}p^{*}w_{i}\\
p^{*}\sum_{i=1}^{2}\hat{x}_{i}+q\hat{y} & > & p^{*}\sum_{i=1}^{2}w_{i}\\
p^{*}\sum_{i=1}^{2}\hat{x}_{i}+p^{*}\hat{v} & > & p^{*}\sum_{i=1}^{2}w_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

since 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}\hat{y}-p^{*}\hat{v}\leq q^{*}y^{*}-p^{*}v^{*}=0\iff q^{*}\hat{y}\leq p^{*}\hat{v}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p^{*}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{2}\hat{x}_{i}+\hat{v}-\sum_{i=1}^{2}w_{i}\right]>0
\]

\end_inset

which contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(\hat{x},\hat{y})$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
The Coase (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

Invariance
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

) Theorem assets that property rights assignments or liability rules have
 no effect on the equilibrium level of an externality (e.g., noise avoidance
 or pollution).
 Consider a two person, two good economy where one good is money, and the
 other is music produces costlessly and enjoyed by person 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

, but disliked by neighbor 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

.
 Both enjoy money.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
What special assumpetions on the two agents preferences validate Coases
 Invariance claim? Justify your answer by considering alternative assignments
 of rights: (1) The musician has a right to play without the neighbor's
 consent; (2) Neighbors consent is required.
 (Compensation may be paid by one party to the other to achieve concesssions.)
 Illustrate your answer by a well-explained diagram.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose both parties have Cobb-Douglas preferences (
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 for money versus music, 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 for money versus absence of music.) Would then the Invariance claim be valid?
 Illustrate your answer by a well-explained diagram.
