\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If preferences are quasi-linear in money 
\begin_inset Formula $(c)$
\end_inset

, consumers can trade disutility of music 
\begin_inset Formula $(m)$
\end_inset

 for money to maintain a given level of utility.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u_{A}=c_{A}+v_{A}(m);\ v_{A}^{\prime}(m)>0\\
u_{B}=c_{B}+v_{B}(m);\ v_{B}^{\prime}(m)<0
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

In this case, everything has a monetary value.
 We also assume decreasing marginal utility in music, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}^{\prime\prime}(m)<0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In general, the competitive equilibrium outcome is not Pareto optimal.
 In order to maximize utility, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 should choose music so that the interior solution satisfies 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{A}^{\prime}(\bar{m})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Even though agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

's utility depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

, he cannot affect the choice of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
On the other hand, the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 will maximize the sum of the consumers' utilities
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{m}v_{A}(m)+v_{B}(m)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition for an interior maximum is:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{A}^{\prime}(m^{*})+v_{B}^{\prime}(m^{*})=0
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $m^{*}$
\end_inset

 is the Pareto optimal amount of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the social optimum is where the sum of the marginal benefit of the
 two consumers equals zero.
 In the case where the externality is bad for consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

, the level of 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{m}>m^{*}$
\end_inset

.
 So, too much 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 is produced.
 Now, we show that, as long as property rights are clearly assigned, the
 two parties will negotiate in such a way that the optimal level of the
 externality-producing activity is implemented.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case 1: The musician has a right to play without the neighbor's consent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 has the right to play as much music as he likes.
 However, this right is contractible.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 can sell agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 the right to prohibit some of his production of music in exchange for some
 transfer, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The two consumers will bargain both over the size of the transfer 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 and over the number of units of the externality good produced 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to determine the outcome of the bargaining, we first specify the
 bargaining mechanism as follows:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 offers agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a take-it-or-leave-it contract specifying a payment 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}$
\end_inset

 and an activity level 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 accepts the offer, than that outcome is implemented.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 does not accept the offer, then agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 can produce as much music as he'd like, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\bar{m}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To analyze this, begin by considering which offers 
\begin_inset Formula $(m,T)$
\end_inset

 will be accepted by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 In the absence of an agreement, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 will produce 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\bar{m}$
\end_inset

, so agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 will accept 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{1},T_{1})$
\end_inset

 if and only if it offers higher utility then 
\begin_inset Formula $m=\bar{m}$
\end_inset

.
 That is, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 accepts if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{A}(m)+T\geq v_{A}(\bar{m})
\]

\end_inset

Given this constraint on the set of acceptable offers, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 will choose 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{1},T_{1})$
\end_inset

 in order to solve the following problem
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{m,T} & v_{B}(m)-T\\
\mbox{s.t.} & v_{A}(m)+T\geq v_{A}(\bar{m})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 prefers higher 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, the constraint will bind at the optimum.
 Thus, the problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{m}}\ v_{B}(m)+v_{A}(\bar{m})-v_{A}(m)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition for this problem is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{A}^{\prime}(m_{1})+v_{B}^{\prime}(m_{1})=0
\]

\end_inset

This is the same condition that defines the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{1}=m^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{1}=v_{A}(\bar{m})-v_{A}(m^{*})$
\end_inset

, and the offer 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{1},T_{1})$
\end_inset

 is accepted by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
\bar under
Case 2: The neighbor's conset is required to play music
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 has the right to prohibit agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 from producing music 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 However, this right is contractible.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 can sell agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 the right to undertake 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{2}$
\end_inset

 units of activity 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 in exchange for some transfer, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 The two consumers will bargain both over the size of the transfer 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

 and over the number of units of the externality good produced, 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
1.
 Agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 offers agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 a take-it-or-leave-it contract specifying a payment 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{2}$
\end_inset

 and an activity level 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
2.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 accepts the offer, than that outcome is implemented.
 If agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 does not accept the offer, then agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 cannot produce any music, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $m=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To analyze this, begin by considering which offers 
\begin_inset Formula $(m,T)$
\end_inset

 will be accepted by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
 In the absence of an agreement, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 must produce 
\begin_inset Formula $m=0$
\end_inset

, so agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 will accept 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 if and only if it offers higher utility than 
\begin_inset Formula $m=0$
\end_inset

.
 That is, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 accepts if and only if
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{A}(m)-T_{2}\geq v_{A}(0)
\]

\end_inset

Given this constraint on the set of acceptable offers, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 will choose 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 in order to solve the following problem:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{m,T} & v_{B}(m)+T\\
\mbox{s.t.} & v_{A}(m)-T\geq v_{A}(0)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 prefers higher 
\begin_inset Formula $T$
\end_inset

, the constraint will bind at the optimum.
 Thus, the problem becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\max_{h}\ v_{B}(m)+v_{A}(m)-v_{A}(0)
\]

\end_inset

The first-order condition for this problem is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{A}^{\prime}(m_{2})+v_{B}^{\prime}(m_{2})=0
\]

\end_inset

This is the same condition that defines the socially optimal level of 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
 Therefore, agent 
\begin_inset Formula $B$
\end_inset

 chooses 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{2}=m^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $T=v_{A}(m^{*})-v_{A}(0)$
\end_inset

, and the offer 
\begin_inset Formula $(m_{2},T_{2})$
\end_inset

 is accepted by agent 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, suppose that both parties have Cobb-Douglas preferences.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For example, consider:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
u_{1}\left(c_{1},m\right)=c_{1}^{\alpha}m^{1-\alpha}\\
u_{2}\left(c_{2},m\right)=c_{2}^{\gamma}m^{1-\gamma}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Unlike the previous case, consumers utility is no longer quasilinear in
 money.
 Consequently, the contract curve is no longer a horizontal line.
 Accordingly, the alternate allocations decribed earlier will each result
 in different equilibrium outcomes.
 Therefore, the invariance theorem may not hold in this case.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Problem
Consider an economy with two firms, one consumer, and three goods: labor
 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

 and two produced goods 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Firm 1's prouciton funciton is 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}=l_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 Firm 2's proudciton function is 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}=q_{1}l_{2}$
\end_inset

 , and it therefore has an externality with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}$
\end_inset

.
 The consumer's utility is 
\begin_inset Formula $u=q_{1}q_{2}$
\end_inset

, and the endowment of labor is 1.
 Let the price of labor be 1.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the competitive equilibrium for the economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find the unique Pareto efficient allocation for the economy.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The government wishes to obtain Pareto efficiency by putting an excise tax
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 on good 2 and giving the money collected back to the consumer as a lump-sum
 subsidy 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 What 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 should be used?
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part a.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the competitive equilibrium, we must find the firms' profit maximizing
 decision and the consumer's utility maximizing decision.
 Note, the price of labor has been normalized to one, and 
\begin_inset Formula $l=l_{1}+l_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Both firms have linear production functions, so in equilibrium, they will
 have zero profit.
 So, if since 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}=l_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}=l_{1}l_{2}$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}=1$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=\frac{1}{l_{1}}$
\end_inset

.
 The consumer has Cobb-Douglas preferences, so his demand function is given
 by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x(p)=\left(\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2p_{1}}\\
\frac{1}{2p_{2}}
\end{array}\right)
\]

\end_inset

By the market clearing condition, we have 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}=x_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}=x_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, the competitive equilibrium is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[x^{*},y_{1}^{*},y_{2}^{*},p^{*}\right]=\left[\left(\begin{array}{c}
\nicefrac{1}{2}\\
\nicefrac{1}{2}\\
0
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
\nicefrac{1}{2}\\
0\\
-\nicefrac{1}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
0\\
\nicefrac{1}{2}\\
-\nicefrac{1}{2}
\end{array}\right),\left(\begin{array}{c}
1\\
2\\
1
\end{array}\right)\right]
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
\bar under
Part b.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the unique Pareto efficient allocation for the economy, we maximize
 the consumer's utility subject to the feasibility constraints
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u=q_{1}q_{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & q_{1}=l_{1}\\
 & q_{2}=q_{1}l_{2}\\
 & l_{1}+l_{2}=1
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

The Lagrangian is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L=l_{1}^{2}l_{2}+\lambda(1-l_{1}-l_{2})
\]

\end_inset

and the first-order conditions are
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2l_{1}l_{2} & = & \lambda\\
l_{1}^{2} & = & \lambda
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Combining these two equations we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2l_{1}l_{2} & = & l_{1}^{2}\\
2l_{2} & = & (1-l_{2})\\
l_{2} & = & \frac{1}{3}\\
l_{1} & = & \frac{2}{3}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, for the Pareto efficient allocation, 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{1}=q_{1}=\frac{2}{3}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{2}=\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}=q_{1}l_{2}=\frac{2}{9}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part c.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The government wishes to obtain Pareto efficiency by putting an excise tax
 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 on good 2 and giving the money collected back to the consumer as a lump-sum
 subsidy 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

.
 So, the consumer's new constrained maximization problem is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max & u_{1}=q_{1}q_{2}\\
\mbox{s.t.} & p_{1}q_{1}+(p_{2}+t)q_{2}=1+s
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the consumer's utility funciton is Cobb-Douglas, his demand for each
 good is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
q_{1} & = & \frac{(1+s)}{2p_{1}}\\
q_{2} & = & \frac{(1+s)}{2p_{2}+2t}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Note, the Pareto optimal allocation has 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}=\frac{2}{3}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{2}=\frac{2}{9}$
\end_inset

, so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\frac{2}{3}=\frac{(1+s)}{2p_{1}}\\
\implies s=\frac{4p_{1}-3}{3}\\
\frac{2}{9}=\frac{(1+s)}{2p_{2}+2t}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

As in part a, if 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}<1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{1}=q_{1}=q_{2}=u=0$
\end_inset

, i.e., the consumer does not maximize his utility.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}>1$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{1}=l_{1}=\infty$
\end_inset

, but this is not feasible.
 So, 
\begin_inset Formula $p=1$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $s=\frac{1}{3}$
\end_inset

.
 Since the consumer receives a subside equal to the amount of tax he pays,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
s & = & q_{2}t\\
\frac{1}{3} & = & \frac{2}{9}t\\
t & = & \frac{3}{2}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Finally, we can find 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}=\frac{3}{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

